<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="gramine biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5467" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-8915 Gramine| is a simple indole alkaloid. It is found in certain wild and cultivated barley lines. Accumulation of gramine in barley is subjected to both developmental and environmental control. The highest content of gramine is found in young leaves, particularly in the first leaf. Each successive leaf contains less gramine. Heat can induce the accumulation of gramine, but only if the leaves were expanding while exposed to high temperature |CITS: [ 16662926]|. In barley, gramine was reported as an antifungal defense compound and is also active against aphids |CITS: [11673626]|. Gramine was also reported toxic to Meadow voles (filed mice) |CITS: [7020159]|. Gramine is synthesized from tryptophan via |FRAME: CPD-8913| (AMI), involving two well-characterized &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-methylation steps |CITS: [7141562][16664431][16930646][21074448]|. The step from trytophan to AMI, which is believed to be the rate-limiting step, is still not well characterized |CITS: [16664431]|.</Description>
